  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "ipc",
      "title": "IPC across the boundary",
      "deck": "Round trips from the frontend to the host and back at 1 KB, 64 KB, 1 MB, 16 MB, on each shell's idiomatic JSON path and on its raw bytes path, plus host-to-frontend push at 1000 events per second. The chart is the idiomatic 1 MB round trip; the headline is the geometric mean over the idiomatic sizes.",
      "unit": "ms",
      "lowerIsBetter": true,
      "verdict": {
        "winnerId": "tauri",
        "headline": "Tauri moved JSON across the boundary 1.44 times faster than Electron over 1 KB, 64 KB, 1 MB and 16 MB (geometric mean of medians)",
        "summary": "Size by size, Tauri over Electron: 1 KB 2.4x slower, 64 KB 1.9x faster, 1 MB 2.1x faster, 16 MB 2.6x faster. The crossover is somewhere under 64 KB: Electron's context bridge is cheaper per call, Tauri's serde is cheaper per byte. Raw bytes tell the same story with smaller gaps (16 MB in 9.4 ms to 15.8). Push at 1,000 events a second: neither shell dropped one, and the p99 gap is a millisecond and a half on both. WKWebView's clock only ticks every millisecond, so the small cells are batched round trips divided out; the batch size is in the run file."
      },
